SHEET(1)                       P32W Unix                        SHEET(1)

NAME
       sheet - a spreadsheet: cells of numbers, text and formulas

SYNOPSIS
       sheet [file]
       sheet -p file

DESCRIPTION
       Under the window manager (wm(1)), sheet shows a sheet of
       cells in a window: 26 columns, A to Z, by 99 rows. As
       much of it shows as the window has room for, six columns
       by fourteen rows to begin with; the window's corner makes
       it bigger or smaller, and it moves over the sheet to keep
       the cell that is picked in sight.

       One cell is picked, the one that is lit. The last line
       tells its name and what was typed into it. What is typed
       begins a new entry for the cell, which shows on the last
       line until it is put in. An entry is one of:

       a number            12.5
       text                anything else: it shows at the left of
                           its cell, cut short at the cell's edge
       a formula           it begins with = and is worked out
                           again whenever a cell is changed

       A cell shows its number, or what its formula comes to, at
       the right; ######## if that is too long for it; #ERR if
       the formula cannot be worked out.

       With -p there is no window: the sheet in the file is
       worked out and printed as it would show, a row a line,
       down to the last row and column that are used.

       It is P8X's sheet brought to this machine. There it is 8
       columns by 12 rows of whole numbers of 16 bits; here the
       numbers are real ones.

FORMULAS
       Numbers, and cells by their names (B7, or b7), with + - *
       and /, brackets, and a - before anything. A cell that is
       empty or has text counts for nothing. And these, of a
       block of cells named by two of its corners (A1:C9), or of
       one cell:

       SUM       its numbers added
       AVG       their average
       MAX, MIN  the greatest and the least of them
       COUNT     how many numbers there are in it

       and of any formula: SQRT, ABS, and INT (the whole number
       at or below it).

              =B2*C2
              =SUM(B2:B9)/COUNT(B2:B9)
              =INT(A1/7)*7

       A formula is #ERR when it is not written rightly, when it
       divides by nothing or takes the root of less than
       nothing, when a cell it uses is #ERR, and when it is
       worked out from itself.

KEYS
       the arrows     pick the cell above, below, left or right
       Return, Tab    the entry is put in, and the cell below
                      (Return) or to the right (Tab) is picked
       ESC            the entry is given up
       Backspace      takes a character of the entry back; with
                      no entry, empties the cell
       ^E             change: the entry begins as what is in the
                      cell
       ^Y             copy the cell's entry
       ^P             paste it into the cell that is picked. A
                      formula's cells are moved as far as it was
                      moved: =B2*C2 copied from D2 is =B5*C5 in D5
       ^O, ^X         save; close (it asks first if the sheet was
                      changed)

THE MOUSE
       on a cell           picks it (an entry being typed is put
                           in first)
       on File or Edit     lets the menu down while the button is
                           held; the item it is let go on is
                           chosen

       The File menu has New, Open, Save, Save as and Close; the
       Edit menu has Change, Empty, Copy and Paste. A file's name
       is asked for on the last line.

FORMAT
       A sheet's file is text, a line for each cell that is not
       empty: its name, a space, and what was typed into it.

              A1 Item
              B1 Cost
              B2 12.5
              B5 =SUM(B2:B4)

EXAMPLES
       win sheet budget
       sheet -p budget
       wm -s /usr/examples/sheet.hand
              a hand plays it: a small budget typed, a formula
              put in with the mouse, another copied and pasted,
              the sheet saved and printed

       It is on the finder's Apps menu.

FILES
       /usr/src/cmd/sheet.c       its source

SEE ALSO
       bc(1), awk(1), finder(1), wm(1), win(1), gfx(3)

DIAGNOSTICS
       sheet: not in a window of wm, when it is run anywhere
       else without -p. What could not be read or written is
       said on the last line.

BUGS
       Every column is ten characters wide.
       One cell at a time is copied, emptied or pasted.
       An entry is 78 characters at most.
       The numbers are this machine's doubles, worked out by
       its library and not by hardware: a big sheet of formulas
       takes a moment.
